      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 269.38,
      "end_seconds": 272.66,
      "duration_seconds": 3.28,
      "text": "Okay, I'll listen to your chest now."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0040",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 289.2,
      "end_seconds": 295.92,
      "duration_seconds": 6.72,
      "text": "There are pink raised wheals on the arms and trunk that blanch with pressure."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0041",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 297.92,
      "end_seconds": 299.06,
      "duration_seconds": 1.14,
      "text": "Okay, doctor."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0042",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 302.98,
      "end_seconds": 332.13,
      "duration_seconds": 29.15,
      "text": "good, you can sit up again okay, so so my impression is this: This is probably chronic spontaneous urticaria a food reaction is still possible i'll request blood tests, it'll help with chronic urticaria it's a routine test and it'll give us a much clearer picture i'll start you on fexofenadine, 180 milligrams, once a day, long term you take it by mouth it's for urticaria"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0043",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 333.04,
      "end_seconds": 334.5,
      "duration_seconds": 1.46,
      "text": "that's simple enough"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0044",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 340.27,
      "end_seconds": 348.81,
      "duration_seconds": 8.54,
      "text": "Keep a diary of foods and and triggers and avoid hot showers. Let's plan a check in six weeks."
    },
